      en: "The Koina river crossing was an important ferry point across the Gambia River, used since the 17th century by traders and travelers. The crossing connected the northern and southern banks and was part of the trade route between Bathurst and the interior. Initially dugout canoes were used, later ferries. The crossing played a key role in the spread of Islam in the 18th century. After a bridge was built at Bansang, the ferry declined but remains a historical site.",
